n, by state. From1999 to 2008 lung cancer incidence among men decreased in 35 of the 44 states analyzed and remained stable in the other nine states. Many of the states with lowest lung cancer incidence, as well as smoking prevalence, were clustered in the West.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":624,"renderedHeight":532,"naturalWidth":624,"naturalHeight":532,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 386 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":75966},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6036a3f3.gif","alt":"The figure includes four maps of the United States that show lung cancer incidence and trends, and smoking behavior among women, by state. From 1999 to 2008, lung cancer incidence among women decreased in six states (California and Nevada [-1.5%], Washington [-1.0%], Florida and Oregon [-0.9%], and Texas [-0.7%]), remained stable in 24 states, and increased slightly in 14 states (not calculated for six states and the District of Columbia). Nearly half of states with higher smoking prevalence for women and more than a third of states with higher lung cancer incidence are in the South.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":623,"renderedHeight":532,"naturalWidth":623,"naturalHeight":532,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 590 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":78301},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/i